<!-- §01 -->
<section id="s-bottleneck" data-code="bottleneck">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Bottleneck</div>
<h2>Why Autoregressive Decoding Is Slow</h2>
<p>
  LLM inference is not compute-bound — it is <strong>memory-bandwidth bound</strong>. Generating each
  token requires loading every model weight from GPU HBM into compute units, performing a
  relatively small matrix-vector product, then writing one token back. On an H100 with 3.35 TB/s
  of memory bandwidth and a 70B parameter model in fp16, that's 140 GB of weight movement just
  to produce a single token. A100s and consumer GPUs fare worse.
</p>
<p>
  The consequence: the actual compute (flops) is done in microseconds, but <em>waiting for weights
  to stream from memory</em> takes milliseconds. Most GPU compute units sit idle throughout.
  Generating a sequence of length 1024 means repeating this wasteful memory round-trip 1024 times
  in strict sequence — no token can start until the previous one is committed.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">sequential vs speculative decoding — memory access pattern</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">tokens/step</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-bottleneck" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Speculative decoding attacks this problem with a deceptively simple observation: <em>verifying a
  proposed token is strictly cheaper than generating one</em>. If a small, fast draft model proposes
  γ tokens ahead, the large target model can verify all γ of them in a single forward pass — the
  same cost as generating one token — by batching the verification queries in parallel.
</p>
<p>
  On average, some draft tokens will be accepted and some rejected. But even a 70% acceptance rate
  on γ=7 draft tokens yields ≈ 5 accepted tokens per target-model forward pass, an immediate
  5× reduction in memory-bound latency.
</p>

<div class="callout key">
  <strong>The key insight:</strong> The target model's forward pass for verification visits the
  same weights once regardless of how many tokens it checks. Batching γ verification queries costs
  the same as checking 1. This asymmetry is what makes speculative decoding possible.
</div>
</section>

<!-- §02 -->
<section id="s-mechanism" data-code="mechanism">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Draft-Verify Mechanism</div>
<h2>Four Phases of Every Speculative Step</h2>
<p>
  At each speculative step, the system alternates between a fast draft phase and a parallel
  verification phase. Understanding this loop precisely is essential before interpreting any
  evaluation metric.
</p>

<div class="step-list" id="step-list">
  <div class="step-box active" id="step-0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Draft: Propose γ Tokens</div>
      <div class="step-desc">
        The draft model runs autoregressively to generate γ candidate tokens
        <em>x̃₁, x̃₂, …, x̃ᵧ</em>, along with the draft probability distributions
        <em>q(·|context)</em> at each position. This is fast because the draft model
        is much smaller (e.g., 0.5B vs 7B parameters).
      </div>
      <div class="step-code">draft_tokens, draft_probs = drafter.generate(ctx, gamma=γ)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="step-1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Verify: One Parallel Target Forward Pass</div>
      <div class="step-desc">
        The large target model processes the original context <em>plus</em> all γ draft tokens
        in a single forward pass — using tree attention if γ > 1. This yields the target
        distributions <em>p(·|context)</em> at each draft position simultaneously.
      </div>
      <div class="step-code">target_probs = target.forward(ctx + draft_tokens)  # ONE pass</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="step-2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Accept / Reject via Speculative Sampling</div>
      <div class="step-desc">
        For each draft token x̃ᵢ, draw u ~ Uniform(0,1). Accept x̃ᵢ if
        u ≤ p(x̃ᵢ) / q(x̃ᵢ). On rejection, resample from the
        adjusted distribution max(0, p − q) / ‖max(0, p − q)‖. This
        provably preserves the target model's output distribution.
      </div>
      <div class="step-code">accept = uniform() &lt;= p(x̃ᵢ) / q(x̃ᵢ)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="step-3">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Commit &amp; Advance Context</div>
      <div class="step-desc">
        All accepted tokens plus one resampled token after the first rejection are
        committed to the output. If n tokens are accepted (n ∈ [0, γ]), the context
        grows by n+1 tokens this step. The expected tokens per step determines
        the acceptance length τ = E[n+1].
      </div>
      <div class="step-code">output.extend(accepted_tokens + [resampled_token])</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <span class="step-prog" id="step-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
  <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
</div>

<p>
  The critical invariant: steps 3 and 4 guarantee the <em>same output distribution as vanilla
  autoregressive decoding</em> from the target model. This is what makes speculative decoding
  "lossless" — not an approximation, but an exact acceleration.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 -->
<section id="s-metrics" data-code="metrics">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Metrics</div>
<h2>What to Measure and Why</h2>
<p>
  The most common mistake in evaluating speculative decoding is reporting only the headline
  speedup number without anchoring it to the underlying mechanism. Two systems with identical
  speedup can have radically different failure modes. Understanding the full metric stack — from
  per-token acceptance rate up to wall-clock latency — is what lets you diagnose a regression.
</p>

<h3>Average Acceptance Length (τ) — The Primary Quality Metric</h3>
<p>
  The average acceptance length τ is the expected number of output tokens committed per
  speculative step. If γ draft tokens are proposed and the per-token acceptance rate is α,
  the geometric series gives:
</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Expected Acceptance Length</div>
  <span class="math-var">τ</span> = E[n + 1] = <span class="math-var">Σ</span><sub>n=0</sub><sup>γ</sup> (n+1) · α<sup>n</sup>(1−α) + (γ+1) · α<sup>γ+1</sup><br>
  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;= (1 − α<sup>γ+1</sup>) / (1 − α) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← simplifies to this</span><br>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment"># Example: α=0.75, γ=6 → τ = (1 − 0.75⁷) / 0.25 ≈ 3.79 tokens/step</span>
</div>
<p>
  Why is τ preferred over raw acceptance rate α? Because τ captures both the per-position
  acceptance rate <em>and</em> the number of draft positions tried. A method that only proposes
  γ=2 tokens with α=0.95 (τ≈1.95) is outperformed by one that proposes γ=7 with α=0.70 (τ≈3.32).
  τ directly predicts throughput improvement.
</p>

<h3>Speedup Ratio — The End-to-End Metric</h3>
<p>
  The theoretical speedup relates τ to c, the cost ratio of a single draft-model forward pass
  relative to a target-model forward pass:
</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Expected Speedup Formula (Leviathan et al., 2023)</div>
  E[speedup] = <span class="math-var">τ</span> / (1 + <span class="math-var">γ</span> · <span class="math-var">c</span>)<br>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment"># τ = avg tokens per step (acceptance length)</span><br>
  <span class="math-comment"># γ = number of draft tokens proposed</span><br>
  <span class="math-comment"># c = (draft model inference time) / (target model inference time)</span><br>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment"># Vicuna 13B + EAGLE-3: τ≈6.6, γ=6, c≈0.04 → speedup ≈ 6.6/1.24 ≈ 5.3×</span>
</div>
<p>
  This formula explains why a small c (cheap draft relative to target) enables higher speedup
  than a higher acceptance rate alone. EAGLE's draft model is roughly 1/25th the cost of the
  target model, making c ≈ 0.04 — so even γ=6 draft steps add only 24% overhead before the
  τ numerator takes effect.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">expected speedup vs acceptance rate α — interactive</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">drag slider</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="ctrl-row">
      <span class="ctrl-label">Draft tokens γ:</span>
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" id="gamma-4">γ=4</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="gamma-6">γ=6</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="gamma-10">γ=10</button>
