      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="c-020">c=0.20</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="canvas-speedup" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Per-Position Acceptance Rate</h3>
<p>
  Acceptance rate is <em>not</em> uniform across draft positions. Position 0 (the first draft
  token) always has the highest acceptance rate since the draft model has access to the full
  ground-truth context. Position 5 has the lowest — the draft model is predicting based on
  its own earlier, potentially wrong, predictions. EAGLE-2 explicitly models this position
  dependence to build smarter draft trees; EAGLE-3 solves the root cause with training-time test.
</p>

<div class="callout warn">
  <strong>Common trap:</strong> Reporting a single "acceptance rate α" hides position collapse.
  A method claiming α=0.80 might have position-0 at 0.95 and position-5 at 0.30 — the headline
  looks good but the 6th draft token is nearly useless. Always report τ (the integrated metric)
  alongside per-position acceptance curves.
</div>

<h3>Throughput vs Latency</h3>
<p>
  Speedup ratio measures per-request latency. At high batch sizes, the picture changes:
  throughput (total tokens/second across all requests) becomes the right metric. These two
  can diverge significantly — a method that halves per-request latency at batch=1 may add
  negligible throughput at batch=32 because GPU compute is now saturated by the draft overhead.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 -->
<section id="s-benchmarks" data-code="benchmarks">
<div class="section-no">04 — Spec-Bench Tasks</div>
<h2>Six Tasks That Cover the Evaluation Surface</h2>
<p>
  Spec-Bench (Xia et al., ACL 2024) was built to solve the reproducibility crisis in speculative
  decoding papers: every paper used different tasks, different temperature settings, and different
  hardware, making side-by-side comparison impossible. Spec-Bench standardizes evaluation to
  six tasks using 80 randomly-selected instances each, measured on a fixed GPU.
</p>

