</table>

<p>
  The spread is dramatic: HumanEval gives EAGLE-3 a τ of 7.54 on Vicuna 13B while Natural
  Questions drops to ~3. This means any single-task evaluation number is misleading — a method
  tuned for code generation tasks will look far better than one tested on open-ended QA.
  <strong>Always report multi-task averages alongside per-task breakdowns.</strong>
</p>

<h3>Why Code and Math Score Higher</h3>
<p>
  Predictability is the core driver. Code contains <code>for</code> loops, indent blocks,
  <code>return</code> statements, and docstring templates that repeat across millions of
  training samples. Given <code>def fizzbuzz(n):</code>, the next five tokens are nearly
  deterministic regardless of context. Similarly, GSM8K answers follow rigid arithmetic
  reasoning chains where many intermediate steps are highly constrained.
</p>
<p>
  Natural Questions and open-domain QA are at the other extreme. The model must recall
  specific facts (names, dates, locations) that depend heavily on context and are
  distributionally rare — exactly the tokens where draft models struggle most.
</p>

