<h3>Distribution-Preserving vs Approximate Evaluation</h3>
<p>
  Spec-Bench uses greedy decoding (temperature=0) as the primary evaluation mode because it
  allows exact output comparison between speculative and autoregressive decoding — the outputs
  must be bit-identical for a lossless method. Temperature>0 requires statistical testing to
  verify distribution preservation, which is rarely done in practice.
</p>
<p>
  Methods like Medusa that use approximate verification (accepting the highest-probability
  token rather than running full rejection sampling) should be explicitly labeled "lossy" in
  evaluations, as they trade a small quality degradation for additional speedup.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 -->
<section id="s-tradeoffs" data-code="tradeoffs">
<div class="section-no">05 — Real-World Tradeoffs</div>
<h2>Five Factors That Shift the Numbers</h2>
<p>
  Publishing a speedup number without context is nearly meaningless. Five key factors each
  shift results by 20–200%, and the best system for one setting can be worst in another.
</p>

<h3>1. Sampling Temperature</h3>
<p>
  Higher temperature makes the target distribution more diffuse — any given token is less
  likely to match the draft model's top prediction. EAGLE-2 at temperature=1 on Vicuna 7B
  achieves 3.05× speedup versus 3.62× at temperature=0: a 15% degradation. Methods with
  weaker draft models can lose 30–50% of their speedup at temperature=1.
</p>

