<p>
  EAGLE-3's advantage at large batch sizes comes from its multi-layer feature fusion — richer
  draft context means fewer rejected tokens, which means the overhead-per-accepted-token stays
  low even when the verification "bargain" shrinks.
</p>

<h3>3. Hardware: Bandwidth vs Compute</h3>
<p>
  Speculative decoding's advantage is fundamentally tied to memory-bandwidth bottlenecking.
  Consumer GPUs (RTX 3090/4090) have lower HBM bandwidth than data-center GPUs (A100/H100),
  which paradoxically can make speculative decoding <em>more</em> beneficial on consumer
  hardware — the target model is more starved for memory bandwidth, so draft+verify savings
  are more impactful. However, consumer GPUs also lack CUDA Graph support in some frameworks,
  adding per-step launch overhead that partially offsets the gain.
</p>
<p>
  Benchmark numbers from Spec-Bench use an A100-80G at batch=1. The same method on a 3090
  will show different absolute speedup ratios. Always report your exact hardware configuration.
</p>

<h3>4. Model Size and Draft/Target Ratio</h3>
<p>
  The cost ratio c = t_draft / t_target is the second most important parameter after α. A
  Vicuna-7B-level draft model used with a 70B target has c ≈ 0.07 — small enough that
  even 7 draft steps only add 49% overhead. Using the same 7B draft with a 13B target raises
  c ≈ 0.40, making speculative decoding far less efficient. The EAGLE family sidesteps this
  by using a purpose-built autoregressive head (≈68M params) rather than a full small model.
</p>

<h3>5. Context Prefix vs Open Generation</h3>
<p>
  Prompt lookup decoding (PLD) achieves 2–3× speedup on summarization tasks where the output
  overlaps heavily with the input prompt. This "free" speedup requires no trained draft model,
  but collapses to near 1× on open-ended generation with no prompt overlap. Always check
  whether a method's numbers rely on input/output lexical similarity.
</p>

<div class="callout info">
  <strong>Evaluation checklist:</strong> Always report (1) hardware and batch size, (2) sampling
  temperature, (3) per-task τ and speedup alongside averages, (4) whether the method is
  lossless or approximate, and (5) the c ratio (draft vs target model size).
</div>
</section>

<!-- §06 -->
<section id="s-evolution" data-code="evolution">
<div class="section-no">06 — Method Evolution</div>
<h2>From 2× to 6×: The EAGLE Ladder</h2>
<p>
  The past two years of speculative decoding research can be read as a series of targeted
  fixes to the acceptance rate ceiling. Each generation identified a specific failure mode in
  the previous approach and addressed it with a focused architectural change.
</p>

