<p style="font-size:.75rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:-.5rem">
  Vicuna 13B, T=0, MT-Bench mean speedup. Bars scaled to EAGLE-3's 5.51×.
</p>

<h3>What Each Generation Fixed</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Standard SpS</strong>: Baseline method. Uses a separate fine-tuned small model as drafter. Limited by distribution mismatch between small and large model families.</li>
  <li><strong>Medusa</strong>: Adds multiple prediction heads to the target model itself. No separate model needed, but heads share the same top-layer representation — limited context diversity.</li>
  <li><strong>EAGLE-1</strong>: Switches to second-to-top-layer features (richer than top-layer logits). Uses a purpose-built autoregressive head with feature+token input. Fixes distribution mismatch by staying within the target model's representation space.</li>
  <li><strong>EAGLE-2</strong>: Discovers that acceptance rate varies with position <em>and</em> context. Draft model confidence scores correlate with actual acceptance probability (conf&lt;0.05 → acc≈0.04, conf&gt;0.95 → acc≈0.98). Builds dynamic draft trees that skip low-confidence branches. ~20–40% faster than EAGLE-1.</li>
  <li><strong>EAGLE-3</strong>: Solves the root cause of position-level acceptance rate collapse. EAGLE's feature prediction creates a training-inference gap — at inference, the model inputs its own predicted features, not the true target features. EAGLE-3 simulates this during training (training-time test), eliminating the distribution shift. Also adds multi-layer feature fusion (low+mid+high target layers) for richer context. Enables scaling law for draft models.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout key">
  <strong>The τ trajectory</strong> tells the full story. On Vicuna 13B (T=0, MT-Bench):
  SpS τ=2.27 → Medusa τ=2.59 → Hydra τ=3.65 → EAGLE τ=3.98 → EAGLE-2 τ=4.83 → EAGLE-3 τ=6.62.
  The speedup scales near-linearly with τ because the denominator (draft overhead) barely changes.
</div>

<h3>Self-Drafting Alternatives</h3>
<p>
  Not every method needs a separate draft model. Self-speculative approaches like LayerSkip
  and REST offer different tradeoffs:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>LayerSkip</strong>: Early-exits at an intermediate layer to draft, then runs the full model for verification. No extra parameters, but acceptance rate is highly sensitive to which layer is used as the exit point.</li>
  <li><strong>REST (Retrieval-based)</strong>: Looks up the current context in a retrieval datastore and uses retrieved text as draft tokens. Requires no training, but performance is highly datastore-dependent. Works exceptionally well on in-domain tasks where the datastore matches the query distribution.</li>
  <li><strong>Prompt Lookup Decoding (PLD)</strong>: Drafts by copying n-grams from the input prompt. Zero overhead, but only helps when output overlaps with input (e.g., summarization, code editing with context).</li>
</ul>

<p>
  When evaluating any self-drafting method, the c ratio in the speedup formula changes
  fundamentally — there's no separate model cost, but there is a computation split cost.
  Compare τ at fixed computational budget to see which is actually better.
</p>
</section>

